Context:
The current handleToolCall in platform/cli/src/commands/tool.ts (lines 117-190) reads JSON exclusively from argv — either the positional [json] argument or the --params <json> flag. Both are subject to the OS ARG_MAX limit (~1 MB on macOS, ~128 KB on older Windows shells). Tools that accept base64-encoded images or large pasted text fail with `zsh: argument list too long: opentabs` before the CLI even starts. The server-side endpoint (platform/mcp-server/src/http-routes.ts handleToolCall, lines 453-528) already accepts arbitrarily large JSON bodies over HTTP POST — there is no server-side limit to fix. This is strictly a CLI input UX bug.

Do NOT adopt the `@filename` prefix pattern the reporter suggested. It's ambiguous: `--params '@foo'` could legitimately be a JSON-encoded string beginning with `@` (e.g. a Twitter-style @mention or an email fragment), and we'd have no way to know whether the user meant 'read file foo' or 'the string @foo'. A separate --params-file flag is unambiguous, matches established CLI patterns (kubectl -f, gh api --input, jq -f, curl --data @file is scoped to curl's own arg parser which operates on a single -d flag), and is self-documenting in --help output.

Current code (lines 117-134 of platform/cli/src/commands/tool.ts):
```
const handleToolCall = async (
  name: string,
  jsonArg: string | undefined,
  options: { port?: number; params?: string; instance?: string; tabId?: number },
): Promise<void> => {
  const port = resolvePort(options);

  // Parse arguments from positional JSON or --params flag (flag takes precedence)
  const rawJson = options.params ?? jsonArg;
  let args: Record<string, unknown> = {};
  if (rawJson) {
    try {
      args = JSON.parse(rawJson) as Record<string, unknown>;
    } catch {
      console.error(pc.red(`Invalid JSON: ${rawJson}`));
      process.exit(2);
    }
  }
```

